# Coding Guidelines: Machine Learning

<purpose>
Follow these guidelines when developing machine learning and data science products.
</purpose>


## 5. Data & AI Lifecycle

### 5.1 Dataset Creation & Management

201. **Label with clear, consistent guidelines**—avoid ambiguity.
202. **Automate data cleaning and validation** with reproducible scripts.
203. **Balance classes to avoid bias** (or account for imbalance explicitly).
204. **Use stratified sampling for splits** when labels are imbalanced.
205. **Track data provenance**—source, date, version.
206. **Anonymize sensitive information where applicable**.
207. **Augment data thoughtfully—avoid introducing noise**.
208. **Use data versioning tools** (e.g., DVC, Quilt, lakeFS).
209. **Ensure real-world diversity is captured in samples**.
210. **Document data assumptions and limitations**.

### 5.2 Model Training & Tuning

211. **Use grid or random search for small spaces**.
212. **Use Bayesian optimization or Optuna for larger spaces**.
213. **Tune only after baseline model is stable**.
214. **Use early stopping to avoid overfitting**.
215. **Log hyperparameters and results systematically**.
216. **Prioritize interpretability vs. performance consciously**.
217. **Quantize models for deployment if latency or size is critical**.
218. **Prune or distill large models for efficiency**.
219. **Experiment on subsets before scaling up**.
220. **Regularize carefully—monitor bias/variance tradeoff**.

### 5.3 Model Evaluation & Fairness

221. **Choose metrics appropriate to the task** (e.g., F1 for imbalance).
222. **Use ROC, PR curves, and confusion matrices to analyze performance**.
223. **Evaluate per group or segment** to uncover bias.
224. **Run ablation studies to understand component contributions**.
225. **Benchmark against simple, interpretable baselines**.
226. **Use human-in-the-loop review for critical outputs**.
227. **Test for stability—minor input changes shouldn't cause major shifts**.
228. **Track performance across versions and time**.
229. **Automate evaluation as part of CI/CD pipeline**.
230. **Simulate edge cases and adversarial inputs explicitly**.
